@meta
id: 30-09
title: The problems panel, read properly
chapter: 30
type: lesson
skills: diagnostics
par: 107
minutes: 8
vscode: the Problems panel, and fixing things top to bottom
vimrc: set errorformat=%-G\ %.%#,%f(%l\\,%c):\ error\ %m suffixesadd=.ts hidden
open: src/domain/order/order.provider.ts

@brief
`tsc.log` is the output of a type-check, provided with this lesson. Five
errors. Three of them are the same sentence about `placedAt` not
existing on `Order`, and the indented line under each one tells you
where the type is declared.

Chapter 19 taught you to load a list like this and walk it. The habit
this lesson is after is the one before that: read the whole list first,
then decide. Three of those five errors are one mistake — the entity
field is called `placed` and every caller was written against
`placedAt`. Rename the field on the type and all three go.

Then the two that are real:

  · the resolver reads `args.orderId` when the argument is `args.id`
  · the payment service compares a status number to the string `'200'`

`order.provider.ts` is correct as it stands, even though three errors
point into it. Leave it, and leave `tsc.log`.

@keys
| `:cgetfile file` | load a list of errors without jumping anywhere |
| `:copen` `:cclose` | show and hide the list |
| `:cc N` | jump to entry number N |
| `%-G` | an `errorformat` pattern for lines to throw away |

@hints
`:cgetfile tsc.log` then `:copen`. Read all five before you touch
anything, and notice how many of them name the same property.
--
The entity is not in the error list at all — the type is fine
TypeScript, it is just the wrong name. Get to it by following the
import at the top of the provider with `gf`.
--
`e` puts you on the last character of `placed`, so `a` then `At` fixes
the field. Then `:cc 4` and `:cc 5` take you to the two real errors.

@solution
    :cgetfile tsc.log<Enter>  :copen<Enter>  :cclose<Enter>
    /order\.entity<Enter> gf  /placed:<Enter> e a At <Esc>
    :cc 4<Enter>  ciw id <Esc>
    :cc 5<Enter>  f' x f' x
    :wqa<Enter>

Five entries, three causes, one of which is not in any of them. That
ratio is normal for a type checker: one broken declaration produces an
error at every use site and none at the declaration. Fixing them top to
bottom means editing three call sites to agree with a field name that
was wrong in the first place.

The `errorformat` for this lesson has two patterns:

    %-G\ %.%#            throw away any line starting with a space
    %f(%l\,%c):\ error\ %m   file, line, column, message

`%-G` is "match this and ignore it". It is how you drop banners,
summaries and, here, the related-information lines that modern
compilers indent under each error. Without it the list has eight
entries, three of which jump you to a declaration you did not ask for.

This is exactly the diagnostics half of a language server. The server
would have told you about `placedAt` as you typed, and it would do it
without a build. What it would not have done is tell you that three of
its own squiggles share one cause. That part is still you.

@start tsc.log
src/domain/order/order.provider.ts(12,31): error TS2551: Property 'placedAt' does not exist on type 'Order'. Did you mean 'placed'?
  src/domain/order/order.entity.ts(6,3): 'placed' is declared here.
src/domain/order/order.provider.ts(16,18): error TS2551: Property 'placedAt' does not exist on type 'Order'. Did you mean 'placed'?
  src/domain/order/order.entity.ts(6,3): 'placed' is declared here.
src/domain/order/order.repository.ts(14,9): error TS2551: Property 'placedAt' does not exist on type 'Order'. Did you mean 'placed'?
  src/domain/order/order.entity.ts(6,3): 'placed' is declared here.
src/domain/order/order.resolver.ts(7,44): error TS2339: Property 'orderId' does not exist on type '{ id: string; }'.
src/infrastructure/services/payment.service.ts(5,9): error TS2367: This comparison appears to be unintentional because the types 'number' and 'string' have no overlap.

@start src/domain/order/order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
  placed: Date;
};

@expected src/domain/order/order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
  placedAt: Date;
};

@start src/domain/order/order.provider.ts
import { Order } from './order.entity';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';

export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(protected orderRepository: OrderRepository) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    return this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });
  }

  public async age(order: Order) {
    return Date.now() - order.placedAt.getTime();
  }

  public async isStale(order: Order) {
    return order.placedAt.getTime() < Date.now() - 86_400_000;
  }
}

@start src/domain/order/order.resolver.ts
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';

export class OrderResolver {
  constructor(protected orderProvider: OrderProvider) {}

  public async order(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    return this.orderProvider.findOne(args.orderId);
  }
}

@expected src/domain/order/order.resolver.ts
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';

export class OrderResolver {
  constructor(protected orderProvider: OrderProvider) {}

  public async order(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    return this.orderProvider.findOne(args.id);
  }
}

@start src/infrastructure/services/payment.service.ts
export class PaymentService {
  public async refund(orderId: string) {
    const response = await this.http.post('/refunds', { orderId });

    if (response.status !== '200') {
      throw exception('REFUND_FAILED');
    }

    return response.data.receiptId;
  }
}

@expected src/infrastructure/services/payment.service.ts
export class PaymentService {
  public async refund(orderId: string) {
    const response = await this.http.post('/refunds', { orderId });

    if (response.status !== 200) {
      throw exception('REFUND_FAILED');
    }

    return response.data.receiptId;
  }
}
